export const B6={title:'Gel electrophoresis and gel interpretation',hook:'Six DNA samples go into six wells of a gel. Thirty minutes later, bands have spread down the gel like rungs of a ladder, and one suspect’s pattern matches the crime scene exactly. Electrophoresis turns invisible molecules into a readable pattern—if the gel is loaded, run and measured correctly.',objectives:['Explain how agarose gel electrophoresis separates DNA by size.','Load and run a gel following HOSA Skill III, including electrode orientation.','Construct a semilog standard curve and estimate fragment sizes (Skill IV).','Interpret gel results for fingerprinting and digestion experiments.'],sections:[
['How electrophoresis separates DNA',[
'**Gel electrophoresis** moves charged molecules through a gel using an electric field. DNA’s phosphate backbone gives it a **negative charge**, so DNA migrates from the **negative electrode (cathode, black)** toward the **positive electrode (anode, red)**—“run to red.” The **agarose** gel is a mesh of polysaccharide fibers; smaller fragments slip through the pores faster and travel farther, while larger fragments move more slowly and stay nearer the wells. Higher agarose percentages (for example 2%) separate small fragments better; lower percentages (0.8%) separate large fragments.',
'A **running buffer** such as **TAE** (Tris-acetate-EDTA) conducts current and maintains pH; it must cover the gel and fill the wells. **Sample loading buffer (SLB)** adds density (glycerol) so samples sink into wells and tracking dyes to monitor progress. A **DNA ladder or size standard** contains fragments of known sizes. After the run, DNA is visualized with a stain such as a DNA-binding fluorescent dye or a blue stain.',
{t:'w',kind:'gel',gel:'fingerprint',title:'Run a DNA fingerprinting gel'}]],
['Loading and running the gel (HOSA Skill III)',[
'HOSA Skill III follows a precise sequence. After PPE, the competitor **collects liquid at the bottom** of the six samples and the DNA size standard by pulse-spinning (balanced) or tapping. Using a 20 µL micropipet with a fresh tip for each tube, they **add 5 µL of sample loading buffer** to all seven tubes and mix. They **place the precast agarose gel** in the chamber with the **wells near the black (−) electrode**, a point judges verify, then **fill the chamber with 1x TAE** to cover the gel and fill the wells. They **load 10 µL of the standard** and of each sample into separate wells, keeping the tip just inside the well without puncturing the gel bottom. They **place the lid** and connect **red to red and black to black**, **run at 100 V**, verbalize “run for 30 minutes,” then verbalize that the run has completed, turn off the power and remove the lid before cleaning up.',
order('Load and run a gel (HOSA Skill III)',['Don PPE and collect sample liquid by pulse-spinning or tapping','Add 5 µL sample loading buffer to each tube with a fresh tip','Place the gel with wells near the black (−) electrode','Fill the chamber with 1x TAE to cover the gel','Load 10 µL standard and each sample into separate wells','Attach the lid: red to red, black to black','Run at 100 V for about 30 minutes','Turn off power, remove the lid and clean up'])]],
['Sizing fragments with a standard curve',[
'Fragment migration is roughly proportional to the **logarithm** of size, so sizes are estimated on **semilog graph paper**. In HOSA Skill IV, the competitor uses a ruler to **measure the distance in millimeters** from the well to each band for the standard and samples, records them in a table, **plots distance (x-axis, linear) versus size (y-axis, logarithmic)** for the standard bands, **draws a line of best fit** and **reads each sample band’s size** from the line. Measure from the bottom of the well to the front edge (or center) of each band consistently.',
{t:'example',title:'Estimate a fragment size',prompt:'Ladder bands: 5,000 bp at 12 mm, 2,000 bp at 24 mm, 1,000 bp at 32 mm, 500 bp at 40 mm. A sample band traveled 28 mm. About how large is it?',steps:['28 mm lies between the 2,000 bp band (24 mm) and the 1,000 bp band (32 mm).','On a log scale, halfway between 2,000 and 1,000 is about 1,400 bp (the geometric mean, √(2,000 × 1,000)).','Reading the line of best fit gives approximately 1,400 bp.'],answer:'About 1,400 bp. Because the scale is logarithmic, the midpoint distance corresponds to the geometric mean, not the arithmetic mean of 1,500 bp.'},
{t:'w',kind:'gel',gel:'sizing',title:'Size an unknown band against the ladder'}]],
['Interpreting results',[
'In a **DNA fingerprinting** experiment, a suspect matches a crime-scene sample only if **every band** appears at the same position. In a **restriction mapping** experiment, the number and sizes of bands reveal how many sites an enzyme cut. **Uncut plasmid** DNA often shows more than one band because plasmids exist in **supercoiled**, **nicked (open circular)** and linear forms that migrate differently; supercoiled DNA usually runs fastest. Smears can indicate degraded DNA or overloaded wells; no bands may mean the DNA was not loaded, the current ran backward (wells at the red electrode, so DNA ran off the top) or staining failed.',
{t:'w',kind:'gel',gel:'digest',title:'Read a plasmid digest'},
kase('Troubleshoot a gel',{a:{text:'After 30 minutes you see no bands at all, and the tracking dye has moved toward the black electrode. What happened?',choices:[['The gel was placed with wells near the red electrode or leads were reversed, so DNA ran out of the gel','b','Correct. DNA moves toward red; reversed orientation sends it the wrong way.',true],['The DNA was too small to see','b','Even small fragments would appear if they stayed in the gel.',false]]},b:{text:'How do you prevent this in Skill III?',choices:[['Place wells at the black (−) electrode and connect red to red, black to black','c','Correct. Judges verify both.',true],['Run at a higher voltage','c','Voltage does not fix reversed orientation.',false]]},c:{text:'You rerun with correct orientation; bands separate clearly and the ladder lets you size each fragment.',end:true}})]],
['Safety and quality',[
'Electrophoresis uses electricity near liquid: keep the lid on during runs, turn off the power before opening, and keep hands dry. Wear gloves when handling stained gels, and follow the stain’s Safety Data Sheet. Record lane order in your notebook before loading, because an unrecorded lane order makes a gel uninterpretable. Run a ladder on every gel, and do not let samples float out of wells by loading too quickly.',
flip('Electrophoresis vocabulary',[['Run to red','DNA moves toward the positive (red) anode'],['TAE buffer','Running buffer that conducts current'],['Loading buffer','Adds density and dye so samples sink and can be tracked'],['DNA ladder','Fragments of known sizes for comparison'],['Semilog plot','Distance (linear) versus size (log scale)'],['Supercoiled plasmid','Tightly coiled form that usually runs fastest']])]],
["Beyond agarose: sequencing and STR profiles",["Agarose gels separate fragments that differ by tens to hundreds of base pairs. To separate fragments differing by a single base, laboratories use **capillary electrophoresis**, in which DNA moves through a thin polymer-filled capillary and fluorescent labels are detected by a laser. This is the basis of **Sanger sequencing**, in which labeled chain-terminating nucleotides create fragments of every length, and of forensic **short tandem repeat (STR)** profiling, in which PCR-amplified repeat regions are sized precisely. The FBI’s CODIS database uses a core set of 20 STR loci for comparing profiles.","Newer **next-generation sequencing** platforms read millions of fragments in parallel without electrophoresis, making whole-genome sequencing affordable. Still, the principles you practice with agarose gels—size standards, careful lane records, controls and pattern comparison—carry into every DNA analysis method."]]
],takeaways:['DNA is negative and runs toward the red (+) electrode; small fragments travel farthest.','Skill III: SLB 5 µL, wells at black, 1x TAE, load 10 µL, red to red, 100 V, about 30 minutes.','Skill IV: measure distances, plot on semilog paper, draw a best-fit line and read sizes; matches require identical band patterns.'],terms:[['Gel electrophoresis','Separation of charged molecules by size in a gel under an electric field.'],['DNA ladder','Mixture of DNA fragments of known sizes.'],['Anode','Positive electrode (red), toward which DNA migrates.']],mcqs:[q('Toward which electrode does DNA migrate?','The positive (red) electrode','The negative (black) electrode','Both equally','Neither','DNA is negatively charged.'),q('In HOSA Skill III, where should the wells be placed?','Near the black (−) electrode','Near the red (+) electrode','In the middle of the chamber','Above the buffer','Judges verify this.'),q('Which fragments travel farthest in an agarose gel?','The smallest','The largest','Supercoiled plasmids always','Unstained fragments','Small fragments pass through pores easily.'),q('What voltage does the HOSA Skill III rating sheet specify?','100 V','10 V','500 V','1,000 V','Run at 100 V for about 30 minutes.'),q('The purpose of sample loading buffer is to:','Make samples dense enough to sink and add tracking dye','Cut DNA','Stain DNA permanently','Conduct current','Glycerol and dyes aid loading.'),q('For Skill IV, distance versus size is plotted on:','Semilog graph paper','A pie chart','A bar graph','Polar coordinates','Migration is proportional to log size.'),q('A suspect’s DNA matches a crime-scene sample when:','Every band is at the same position','At least one band matches','The lanes are next to each other','The bands are brighter','A match requires the full pattern.'),q('Uncut plasmid DNA often shows multiple bands because:','Plasmids exist in supercoiled, nicked and linear forms','The enzyme cut it','The ladder contaminated it','Buffer was missing','Conformations migrate differently.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to load and run an agarose gel in HOSA Skill III, then explain how you would use a DNA ladder and semilog graph to estimate fragment sizes in Skill IV and decide whether a suspect matches a crime-scene sample.',rubric:[['Skill III steps and orientation',['loading buffer','black','tae','red to red','100 v','30 minutes']],['Principle of separation',['negative','red','smaller','farther','agarose']],['Skill IV standard curve',['measure','mm','semilog','line of best fit','ladder']],['Interpretation of a match',['every band','same position','pattern','match']]],model:'After PPE, I would pulse-spin the samples and standard, add 5 µL of sample loading buffer to each with a fresh tip, place the gel with the wells near the black negative electrode and fill the chamber with 1x TAE to cover it. I would load 10 µL of the standard and each sample, attach the lid red to red and black to black and run at 100 V for about 30 minutes. DNA is negatively charged and moves toward the red electrode, with smaller fragments traveling farther through the agarose. For Skill IV, I would measure the distance from each well to each band in millimeters, plot the ladder’s distance against size on semilog paper, draw a line of best fit and read each sample band’s size. A suspect matches the crime scene only if every band appears at the same positions.'},sources:[RULES,NHGRI_GEL,BIORAD]};

export const B7={title:'Bacterial transformation and transformation efficiency',hook:'After a heat shock lasting only 50 seconds, a few bacteria in a tube take up a plasmid carrying a jellyfish gene. The next day, under ultraviolet light, their colonies glow green. Transformation is one of biotechnology’s most important techniques—and HOSA tests it as both a hands-on skill and a calculation.',objectives:['Explain plasmids, selectable markers and gene regulation in the pGLO system.','Perform the steps of a heat-shock transformation following HOSA Skill VI.','Predict growth and fluorescence on LB, LB/amp and LB/amp/ara plates.','Calculate transformation efficiency with correct units (Skill VII).'],sections:[
['Plasmids and selection',[
'**Transformation** is the uptake of foreign DNA by a cell, causing a genetic change. In biotechnology, bacteria are transformed with **plasmids**—small circular DNA molecules that replicate independently. A useful plasmid carries an **origin of replication**, a **selectable marker** such as the **bla** gene for **beta-lactamase**, which confers **ampicillin resistance**, and a **gene of interest**. The Bio-Rad **pGLO** plasmid carries the gene for **green fluorescent protein (GFP)**, originally from the jellyfish *Aequorea victoria*, under control of the **arabinose operon**: the **araC** protein switches on GFP expression only when the sugar **arabinose** is present. This lets students see gene regulation directly.',
'Only a small fraction of cells take up plasmid DNA. Plating cells on **LB agar with ampicillin** selects transformants, because cells without the plasmid cannot break down the antibiotic and do not grow.',
match('Match each plasmid feature',[['Gene for beta-lactamase','Ampicillin resistance (selectable marker)'],['Green fluorescent protein gene','Visible reporter of expression'],['araC regulator','Turns on GFP when arabinose is present'],['Origin of replication','Lets the plasmid copy itself in the cell'],['Arabinose','Sugar that induces GFP expression']])]],
['The heat-shock procedure (HOSA Skill VI)',[
'Bacterial cell membranes are not naturally permeable to plasmids, so cells are made **competent**. In HOSA Skill VI, after PPE, the competitor labels tubes **+pGLO** and **−pGLO**, pipets **250 µL of transformation solution (0.05 M CaCl₂)** into each with a 100–1,000 µL micropipet and places them **on ice**. Using a **sterile loop**, they scrape **2–4 E. coli colonies** from the starter plate into the +pGLO tube, disperse them, close it and return it to ice, then repeat with a new loop for the −pGLO tube, disposing of each loop properly. Following the Bio-Rad protocol, plasmid DNA is added to the +pGLO tube only, both tubes are incubated on ice, given a brief **heat shock at 42 °C** (about 50 seconds), returned to ice, then given **LB broth** to recover before cells are spread on plates and incubated. Calcium ions help shield negative charges on DNA and membranes, and the cold-then-hot temperature change helps DNA enter.',
"Timing and temperature matter. Cells must be kept cold before the heat shock so membranes are rigid; the brief 42 °C pulse creates a temperature gradient that helps plasmid DNA cross; returning tubes to ice immediately stops the shock. Heat-shocking too long kills cells, and skipping the ice steps lowers efficiency. The **recovery** period in LB broth lets cells repair their membranes and begin making beta-lactamase before they meet ampicillin on the plate. Spreading cells evenly with a sterile spreader and keeping plates upside down during incubation complete the procedure. Record which tube went onto which plate, because the four-plate comparison depends on correct labels.",
order('Transformation steps (Bio-Rad pGLO sequence)',['Label +pGLO and −pGLO tubes','Add 250 µL CaCl₂ transformation solution to each and place on ice','Transfer 2–4 colonies into each tube with separate sterile loops','Add plasmid to the +pGLO tube only and incubate on ice','Heat shock at 42 °C for about 50 seconds','Return to ice, then add LB broth to recover','Spread cells on the labeled plates and incubate'])]],
['Predicting results',[
'Four plates show the logic. **−pGLO on LB**: a lawn of growth (no antibiotic). **−pGLO on LB/amp**: no growth (no resistance). **+pGLO on LB/amp**: colonies grow (resistant transformants) but do not glow because arabinose is absent. **+pGLO on LB/amp/ara**: colonies grow and **glow green under UV light** because arabinose turns on GFP. The −pGLO plates act as **controls** showing that ampicillin kills untransformed cells and that the cells were alive.',
sort('Predict each plate',['Growth, no glow','Growth and glowing','No growth'],[['−pGLO on LB',0],['+pGLO on LB/amp',0],['+pGLO on LB/amp/ara',1],['−pGLO on LB/amp',2],['−pGLO on LB/amp/ara',2]])]],
['Calculating transformation efficiency (Skill VII)',[
'**Transformation efficiency** measures how many cells were transformed per microgram of plasmid DNA: efficiency = **number of transformed colonies ÷ micrograms of DNA spread on the plate**, reported in **transformants per µg** (or CFU/µg). HOSA Skill VII asks competitors to count colonies (estimating from a quadrant if there are many), calculate the micrograms of DNA spread, express it in µg, calculate efficiency and express it with correct units.',
'To find DNA spread: total DNA used = concentration × volume (for example 0.08 µg/µL × 10 µL = 0.8 µg); fraction spread = volume spread ÷ total volume of cell suspension (for example 100 µL ÷ 510 µL ≈ 0.196); DNA spread = total DNA × fraction (0.8 × 0.196 ≈ 0.157 µg). With 120 colonies, efficiency ≈ 120 ÷ 0.157 ≈ 765 transformants/µg. Always use the values in your scenario.',
{t:'w',kind:'transformation',title:'Calculate transformation efficiency'}]],
['Gene regulation and applications',[
'The pGLO system models how bacteria control genes. In the natural **arabinose operon**, the araC protein represses transcription of genes for arabinose digestion until arabinose is present; then it activates them. Replacing those genes with GFP lets students see activation. Transformation is used to copy plasmids, produce proteins such as insulin in bacteria and introduce genes into many organisms. Safety: classroom E. coli strains are BSL-1, but plates are treated as biohazard waste and are not opened after incubation except as directed.',
kase('Unexpected plate results',{a:{text:'Your −pGLO LB/amp plate shows many colonies. What does this suggest?',choices:[['The ampicillin was inactive or the plate was contaminated or mislabeled','b','Correct. Without the plasmid, cells should not grow on ampicillin.',true],['The experiment worked perfectly','b','Growth here means the control failed.',false]]},b:{text:'Your +pGLO LB/amp/ara plate has colonies but none glow. Which is the best explanation?',choices:[['Arabinose was missing or degraded, or the plates were not viewed under UV light','c','Correct. GFP expression requires arabinose and is seen under UV.',true],['The plasmid lacks an ampicillin resistance gene','c','Colonies grew on ampicillin, so resistance is present.',false]]},c:{text:'You check plate labels, prepare fresh plates and repeat; the controls now behave as expected and transformed colonies glow on arabinose.',end:true}})]],
["Other ways to deliver DNA",["Heat shock with calcium chloride is one of several gene-delivery methods. **Electroporation** applies a brief electrical pulse that opens temporary pores in membranes and often gives higher efficiency. **Transfection** introduces DNA into eukaryotic cells using lipid carriers or chemicals. **Viral vectors**, such as modified adeno-associated viruses or lentiviruses, deliver genes in gene therapy. Plant scientists use *Agrobacterium tumefaciens*, which naturally transfers DNA into plant cells, or a **gene gun** that shoots DNA-coated particles.","Genome editing tools such as **CRISPR-Cas9** use a guide RNA to direct the Cas9 nuclease to a matching DNA sequence, where it cuts both strands; the cell’s repair processes can then disable a gene or insert a new sequence. All of these methods still rely on the ideas in this lesson: getting DNA into cells, selecting cells that received it, and confirming the change worked."]]
],takeaways:['pGLO carries ampicillin resistance (bla) and GFP controlled by araC and arabinose.','Skill VI: 250 µL CaCl₂ on ice, 2–4 colonies per tube with separate loops, plasmid in +pGLO, heat shock 42 °C, recovery in LB.','Efficiency = colonies ÷ µg DNA spread, in transformants/µg; DNA spread = total DNA × fraction of suspension plated.'],terms:[['Transformation','Uptake and expression of foreign DNA by a cell.'],['Selectable marker','A gene, such as ampicillin resistance, that allows only transformed cells to grow.'],['Transformation efficiency','Transformed colonies per microgram of DNA spread.']],mcqs:[q('In the pGLO system, what turns on GFP expression?','Arabinose binding to the araC regulator','Ampicillin','Heat shock alone','UV light','araC activates GFP when arabinose is present.'),q('Which plate shows glowing colonies?','+pGLO on LB/amp/ara','+pGLO on LB/amp','−pGLO on LB','−pGLO on LB/amp','GFP requires arabinose.'),q('Which plate should show no growth?','−pGLO on LB/amp','−pGLO on LB','+pGLO on LB/amp','+pGLO on LB/amp/ara','Untransformed cells are killed by ampicillin.'),q('In HOSA Skill VI, how much transformation solution goes into each tube?','250 µL','25 µL','2.5 mL','10 µL','A 100–1,000 µL pipet is set to 250 µL.'),q('The transformation solution in the HOSA scenario is:','0.05 M CaCl₂','1 M NaCl','70% ethanol','TAE buffer','Calcium ions aid DNA uptake.'),q('Transformation efficiency is expressed in:','Transformants per µg of DNA','Colonies per mL','µg per colony','Volts per cm','Skill VII checks units.'),q('If 0.8 µg of DNA is in 510 µL and 100 µL is spread, about how much DNA is spread?','0.157 µg','0.8 µg','80 µg','0.016 µg','0.8 × (100/510).'),q('Why use a separate sterile loop for each tube?','To avoid contamination between tubes','To add more DNA','To heat the cells','To make colonies glow','Loops are disposed of after one use.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how pGLO transformation works from competent cells to glowing colonies, predict results on four plates, and calculate transformation efficiency if 150 colonies grow when 0.8 µg of plasmid is in 510 µL and 100 µL is spread.',rubric:[['Procedure: CaCl₂, ice, heat shock, recovery',['cacl','ice','heat shock','42','lb broth','recover']],['Selection and regulation',['ampicillin','bla','gfp','arabinose','arac']],['Four-plate predictions',['lawn','no growth','glow','lb/amp/ara']],['Efficiency calculation with units',['0.157','955','956','transformants/µg','cfu/µg']]],model:'Cells are made competent in cold 0.05 M CaCl₂ on ice, colonies are added, plasmid is added to the +pGLO tube, and a 42 °C heat shock followed by ice and LB recovery helps DNA enter. The plasmid’s bla gene gives ampicillin resistance, and GFP is expressed only when arabinose binds araC. The −pGLO cells grow as a lawn on LB but not on LB/amp, while +pGLO cells grow on LB/amp and glow green under UV only on LB/amp/ara. DNA spread = 0.8 µg × (100 ÷ 510) ≈ 0.157 µg, so efficiency = 150 ÷ 0.157 ≈ 956 transformants/µg.'},sources:[RULES,BIORAD,NHGRI_PLASMID]};

export const B8={title:'Polymerase chain reaction (PCR)',hook:'From a single strand of hair or a nasal swab containing a few copies of DNA, PCR can make billions of copies in about two hours. That amplification made modern genetic testing, forensic DNA profiling and rapid infectious disease testing possible.',objectives:['Explain the purpose and components of PCR.','Describe the three temperature steps of each cycle and why each matters.','Calculate the number of copies after a number of cycles.','Compare variations such as RT-PCR and real-time quantitative PCR, and apply contamination controls.'],sections:[
['What PCR does',[
'The **polymerase chain reaction (PCR)**, developed by Kary Mullis in 1983 (Nobel Prize 1993), copies a specific segment of DNA many times. A PCR reaction contains the **template DNA**, two short single-stranded **primers** that flank the target region (one for each strand), the four **deoxynucleotide triphosphates (dNTPs)**, a **heat-stable DNA polymerase** such as **Taq polymerase** from the hot-spring bacterium *Thermus aquaticus*, **magnesium ions** (Mg²⁺, a polymerase cofactor) and buffer. The reaction runs in a **thermal cycler** that rapidly changes temperature. Taq’s heat stability was the breakthrough that allowed automated cycling without adding fresh enzyme each cycle.',
match('Match each PCR component',[['Short sequences that define the target region','Primers'],['Heat-stable enzyme that builds new strands','Taq polymerase'],['Building blocks A, T, G and C','dNTPs'],['Cofactor required by the polymerase','Magnesium ions'],['Machine that changes temperatures rapidly','Thermal cycler'],['The DNA containing the target sequence','Template']])]],
['The three steps of a cycle',[
'Each **cycle** has three steps. **Denaturation** at about **94–95 °C** breaks the hydrogen bonds between strands, separating the double helix. **Annealing** at a lower temperature, typically **50–65 °C** depending on the primers, lets primers bind (anneal) to complementary sequences on each strand. **Extension** (elongation) at **72 °C**, Taq’s optimal temperature, lets the polymerase add dNTPs to the 3′ end of each primer, building a new complementary strand. An initial longer denaturation and a final extension are usually added. A run of 25–35 cycles takes roughly one to two hours.',
order('Order the steps of one PCR cycle',['Denaturation at about 94–95 °C separates strands','Annealing at about 50–65 °C binds primers','Extension at 72 °C builds new strands with Taq polymerase'])]],
['Exponential amplification',[
'Ideally, the number of target copies doubles each cycle, so after *n* cycles one double-stranded molecule produces **2ⁿ** copies. After 10 cycles there are 2¹⁰ = 1,024 copies; after 30 cycles, 2³⁰ ≈ 1.07 billion. Real reactions plateau as primers and dNTPs are used up and enzyme activity declines. Starting with more template reduces the cycles needed to reach detection—the principle behind quantitative PCR.',
{t:'example',title:'How many copies?',prompt:'A reaction starts with 50 copies of a target. Assuming perfect doubling, how many copies are present after 20 cycles?',steps:['Copies = starting copies × 2ⁿ.','2²⁰ = 1,048,576.','50 × 1,048,576 = 52,428,800.'],answer:'About 52.4 million copies. In practice, efficiency is below 100% and reactions plateau.'},
{t:'check',q:'Starting from one DNA molecule with perfect doubling, how many copies exist after 5 cycles?',options:['32','10','25','64'],why:'2⁵ = 32.'}]],
['Variations and uses',[
'**Reverse transcription PCR (RT-PCR)** first uses the enzyme **reverse transcriptase** to make complementary DNA (cDNA) from RNA, allowing detection of RNA viruses such as SARS-CoV-2 or measurement of gene expression. **Real-time quantitative PCR (qPCR)** measures fluorescence during each cycle; the **cycle threshold (Ct)** is the cycle at which fluorescence crosses a set level, and a **lower Ct means more starting target**. PCR is used in infectious disease diagnosis, genetic testing, forensic **short tandem repeat (STR)** profiling, paternity testing, food safety and research cloning. PCR products can be checked by gel electrophoresis.',
"During the COVID-19 pandemic, RT-qPCR became the reference method for diagnosing SARS-CoV-2 infection: a swab sample was processed to extract RNA, reverse transcriptase made cDNA, and primers and fluorescent probes targeted viral genes. A sample was reported positive when its fluorescence crossed the threshold within a set number of cycles. Because PCR detects genetic material, it can stay positive after a person is no longer infectious, which is why results are interpreted alongside symptoms and timing. Similar PCR panels identify many respiratory, sexually transmitted and bloodborne pathogens, often faster than culture.",
sort('Which technique fits?',['Standard PCR','RT-PCR','qPCR'],[['Amplify a gene to clone into a plasmid',0],['Copy STR regions for forensic profiling',0],['Detect an RNA virus by first making cDNA',1],['Measure how much target is present from the cycle threshold',2],['Compare gene expression levels in real time',2]])]],
['Controls and contamination',[
'Because PCR copies tiny amounts of DNA, contamination can create false positives. Laboratories separate **pre-PCR** and **post-PCR** areas, use **filter pipet tips**, change gloves, prepare master mixes, keep tubes closed and include controls. A **negative control (no-template control)** contains everything except template DNA and should show no product; a band means contamination. A **positive control** contains known target DNA and should show product; no band means the reaction failed. Primer design matters: primers should be specific to the target and have similar melting temperatures.',
kase('Interpreting PCR controls',{a:{text:'Your no-template control shows a bright band of the expected size. What does this indicate?',choices:[['Contamination with target DNA, so results are unreliable','b','Correct. The no-template control should have no product.',true],['The reaction worked better than expected','b','Any product in the negative control signals contamination.',false]]},b:{text:'What should the lab do next?',choices:[['Discard results, clean work areas, use fresh reagents and repeat with controls','c','Correct. Results cannot be reported until controls pass.',true],['Report the patient samples anyway','c','Contaminated runs cannot be trusted.',false]]},c:{text:'After cleaning and using fresh reagents, the no-template control is blank, the positive control shows a band, and sample results are interpretable.',end:true}})]],
["Designing primers and checking products",["Primers are typically **18–25 nucleotides** long, match sequences flanking the target, and point toward each other so the region between them is copied. Good primers have about **40–60% G–C content**, similar **melting temperatures** (Tm), and no strong complementarity to themselves or each other, which would form **primer dimers**. The annealing temperature is usually set a few degrees below the primers’ Tm: too low allows nonspecific binding and extra products; too high prevents binding and gives no product.","After PCR, products are often checked by **agarose gel electrophoresis** alongside a ladder. A single band of the expected size suggests specific amplification; extra bands suggest nonspecific binding; a faint band near the bottom may be primer dimers; no band may mean failed reaction, wrong annealing temperature or missing template. In a classroom genotyping experiment, the presence or absence of a band of a certain size reveals which version of a gene a sample carries.",{"t":"w","kind":"gel","gel":"sizing","title":"Size a PCR product against the ladder"}]]
],takeaways:['PCR needs template, two primers, dNTPs, Mg²⁺ and heat-stable Taq polymerase in a thermal cycler.','Each cycle: denature about 94–95 °C, anneal about 50–65 °C, extend at 72 °C; copies ≈ starting copies × 2ⁿ.','RT-PCR detects RNA; qPCR uses Ct (lower = more target); no-template and positive controls detect contamination and failure.'],terms:[['Primer','Short single-stranded DNA that binds next to the target and starts synthesis.'],['Taq polymerase','Heat-stable DNA polymerase from Thermus aquaticus.'],['Cycle threshold (Ct)','qPCR cycle at which fluorescence crosses a threshold.']],mcqs:[q('What is the purpose of the denaturation step?','Separate the two DNA strands','Bind primers','Build new strands','Cut DNA','High heat breaks hydrogen bonds.'),q('At what temperature does Taq polymerase extend new strands?','72 °C','37 °C','95 °C','4 °C','72 °C is Taq’s optimum.'),q('Why is Taq polymerase used in PCR?','It is stable at high temperatures','It cuts DNA at specific sites','It glows green','It makes RNA from DNA','It survives repeated denaturation.'),q('Starting from one molecule, perfect doubling for 10 cycles gives:','1,024 copies','20 copies','100 copies','10,000 copies','2¹⁰ = 1,024.'),q('Which technique first converts RNA into cDNA?','RT-PCR','Standard PCR','Gel electrophoresis','ELISA','Reverse transcriptase makes cDNA.'),q('In qPCR, a lower Ct value means:','More starting target','Less starting target','The reaction failed','Contamination always','Fluorescence crosses the threshold sooner.'),q('A no-template control showing a band indicates:','Contamination','Successful amplification of the sample','Primer failure','Too little Taq','The control should be blank.'),q('Who developed PCR?','Kary Mullis','James Watson','Rosalind Franklin','Louis Pasteur','He developed it in 1983.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the components and temperature steps of PCR, calculate the copies made from 100 starting molecules after 15 cycles, and explain how a laboratory uses controls to trust a positive result.',rubric:[['Components',['primer','dntp','taq','template','magnesium']],['Three steps with temperatures',['denatur','94','anneal','50','extension','72']],['Calculation',['32,768','3,276,800','2^15','2¹⁵']],['Controls',['no-template','negative control','positive control','contamination']]],model:'PCR combines template DNA, two primers flanking the target, dNTPs, magnesium ions and heat-stable Taq polymerase in a thermal cycler. Each cycle denatures DNA at about 94–95 °C, anneals primers at about 50–65 °C and extends new strands at 72 °C. With perfect doubling, 100 molecules after 15 cycles give 100 × 2¹⁵ = 100 × 32,768 = 3,276,800 copies. A laboratory trusts a positive result only if the no-template negative control shows no product, ruling out contamination, and the positive control shows the expected product, confirming the reaction worked.'},sources:[RULES,NHGRI_PCR,BIORAD]};

export const B9={title:'Proteins: structure, function and the Bradford assay',hook:'A protein’s job depends on its shape: an antibody’s tips fit a virus, an enzyme’s pocket fits its substrate. Biotechnology companies spend enormous effort making proteins fold correctly and measuring how much they have made. The Bradford assay, a HOSA skill, turns protein concentration into a color you can measure.',objectives:['Describe amino acids and the four levels of protein structure.','Explain how proteins function as enzymes, antibodies and signaling molecules, and how denaturation affects them.','Describe protein analysis methods including SDS-PAGE and spectrophotometry.','Perform and analyze a Bradford protein assay following HOSA Skill V.'],sections:[
['Building blocks and structure',[
'**Proteins** are polymers of **amino acids** joined by **peptide bonds**. There are 20 common amino acids, each with an amino group, a carboxyl group and a distinctive **R group** that makes it nonpolar, polar or charged. Proteins have four levels of structure. **Primary** structure is the amino acid sequence, determined by the gene. **Secondary** structure includes **alpha helices** and **beta sheets** stabilized by hydrogen bonds in the backbone. **Tertiary** structure is the overall three-dimensional fold, stabilized by interactions among R groups, including hydrophobic interactions, ionic bonds, hydrogen bonds and **disulfide bridges** between cysteines. **Quaternary** structure is the arrangement of multiple polypeptide chains, as in hemoglobin’s four subunits or an antibody’s two heavy and two light chains.',
match('Match the level of structure',[['Sequence of amino acids','Primary'],['Alpha helices and beta sheets','Secondary'],['Overall three-dimensional fold of one chain','Tertiary'],['Assembly of multiple polypeptide chains','Quaternary'],['Covalent bond between cysteine residues','Disulfide bridge'],['Bond linking amino acids','Peptide bond']])]],
['Function and denaturation',[
'Proteins act as **enzymes** that speed reactions by lowering activation energy, **structural** proteins such as collagen, **transport** proteins such as hemoglobin, **antibodies** that recognize antigens, **hormones** such as insulin, and **receptors**. Function depends on shape. **Denaturation**—loss of three-dimensional structure caused by heat, extreme pH, detergents or certain chemicals—usually destroys function without breaking peptide bonds. That is why enzymes are kept cold and why biotechnology processes control temperature and pH carefully. Enzyme activity also depends on substrate concentration and inhibitors.',
'Biotechnology uses many proteins: enzymes in detergents and food processing, therapeutic proteins and antibodies, and research tools such as restriction enzymes and Taq polymerase. Making them in cells, then **purifying** them by methods such as **chromatography** (separating by size, charge or binding affinity), is central to biomanufacturing.']],
['Analyzing proteins',[
'**SDS-PAGE** (sodium dodecyl sulfate–polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis) separates proteins by size. SDS denatures proteins and coats them with negative charge so they all migrate toward the positive electrode, with smaller proteins traveling farther, like DNA in agarose. A protein ladder estimates size in kilodaltons (kDa). **Western blots** transfer separated proteins to a membrane and detect a specific protein with antibodies.',
'**Spectrophotometry** measures how much light a sample absorbs. Under **Beer’s law**, absorbance is proportional to concentration (and path length), so a **standard curve** made from known concentrations lets you determine an unknown concentration. Proteins absorb ultraviolet light at 280 nm, but colorimetric assays such as the **Bradford assay** are common for teaching and routine work.',
sort('Which method fits the goal?',['SDS-PAGE','Western blot','Bradford assay','Chromatography'],[['Separate proteins by size on a gel',0],['Detect one specific protein with antibodies after separation',1],['Measure total protein concentration with a color change',2],['Purify a protein by size, charge or binding',3]])]],
['The Bradford assay (HOSA Skill V)',[
'The **Bradford assay** uses **Coomassie Brilliant Blue G-250** dye, which shifts from brown to **blue** when it binds protein; absorbance is read at **595 nm**. In HOSA Skill V, the competitor dons PPE, labels two tubes for **1/50 dilutions** of samples A and B, pipets **2 µL of each sample** with a 20 µL micropipet and **98 µL of 1x PBS** with a 20–200 µL micropipet, and mixes well. They label **eight cuvettes** for the **blank** and **protein standards** (0.125, 0.250, 0.500, 0.750, 1.000, 1.500 and 2.000 mg/mL), pipet **20 µL of PBS** into the blank and **20 µL of each standard** into its cuvette with clean tips, and add samples to their cuvettes. They then add **1 mL of 1x Bradford reagent** to all ten cuvettes with a 100–1,000 µL micropipet and fresh tips, mixing gently by pipetting up and down without drawing liquid into the pipet barrel, and allow the color to develop before reading.',
order('Bradford assay sequence (HOSA Skill V)',['Don PPE and label two tubes for 1/50 dilutions','Mix 2 µL of each sample with 98 µL of 1x PBS','Label cuvettes: blank and seven standards','Add 20 µL PBS to the blank and 20 µL of each standard','Add the diluted samples to their cuvettes','Add 1 mL 1x Bradford reagent to every cuvette and mix gently','Incubate, then read absorbance at 595 nm','Build a standard curve and calculate concentrations'])]],
['Using the standard curve',[
'After the blank zeroes the spectrophotometer, absorbance of each standard is plotted (x = concentration, y = absorbance) and a best-fit line drawn. Each diluted sample’s concentration is read from the curve, then **multiplied by the dilution factor**—50 for a 1/50 dilution—to get the original concentration. Samples whose absorbance falls outside the standard range should be diluted differently and remeasured.',
{t:'example',title:'Find a sample’s concentration',prompt:'A standard curve gives absorbance = 0.60 × concentration (mg/mL). Diluted sample A (1/50) reads 0.36 at 595 nm. What is the original concentration?',steps:['Concentration of the diluted sample = 0.36 ÷ 0.60 = 0.60 mg/mL.','Multiply by the dilution factor: 0.60 × 50 = 30 mg/mL.'],answer:'Sample A contains about 30 mg/mL protein. Forgetting the dilution factor would underestimate it fiftyfold.'},
flip('Protein vocabulary',[['Peptide bond','Covalent bond between amino acids'],['Denaturation','Loss of 3D shape and function'],['SDS-PAGE','Separates proteins by size'],['Beer’s law','Absorbance is proportional to concentration'],['Coomassie dye','Turns blue when bound to protein'],['Dilution factor','Multiply diluted result by this to get original concentration']])]],
["Enzymes at work in biotechnology",["Enzymes are proteins that catalyze reactions by binding **substrates** at an **active site** and lowering activation energy. Each enzyme has an **optimal temperature and pH**; activity rises with temperature until denaturation causes a sharp drop. **Competitive inhibitors** compete with substrate for the active site, while **noncompetitive inhibitors** bind elsewhere and change the enzyme’s shape. Adding more substrate increases reaction rate until the enzyme is **saturated**.","Biotechnology depends on enzymes: restriction enzymes and ligase build recombinant DNA; Taq polymerase drives PCR; reverse transcriptase makes cDNA; proteases in detergents remove protein stains; amylases convert starch to sugars in food and biofuel production; and enzymes such as horseradish peroxidase generate color in ELISAs. Choosing conditions that keep enzymes folded and active—correct buffer, temperature and storage on ice—is a practical skill you use in nearly every protocol."]]
],takeaways:['Proteins have primary, secondary, tertiary and quaternary structure; shape determines function, and denaturation destroys it.','SDS-PAGE separates by size; Beer’s law links absorbance and concentration through a standard curve.','Skill V: 1/50 dilution (2 µL + 98 µL PBS), blank and seven standards at 20 µL, 1 mL Bradford reagent, read at 595 nm, multiply by 50.'],terms:[['Amino acid','Monomer of proteins with a variable R group.'],['Standard curve','Graph of known concentrations used to determine unknowns.'],['Bradford assay','Colorimetric protein assay using Coomassie dye read at 595 nm.']],mcqs:[q('Alpha helices and beta sheets are examples of which level of protein structure?','Secondary','Primary','Tertiary','Quaternary','They form from backbone hydrogen bonds.'),q('Denaturation of a protein usually:','Destroys its 3D shape and function','Breaks all peptide bonds','Changes its amino acid sequence','Makes it more active','Primary structure remains.'),q('In the Bradford assay, Coomassie dye bound to protein turns:','Blue','Red','Clear','Green','Absorbance is read at 595 nm.'),q('A 1/50 dilution in HOSA Skill V is made with:','2 µL sample + 98 µL PBS','50 µL sample + 50 µL PBS','1 µL sample + 50 µL PBS','98 µL sample + 2 µL PBS','Total 100 µL, 1 part in 50.'),q('How much Bradford reagent is added to each cuvette in Skill V?','1 mL','20 µL','98 µL','10 mL','A 100–1,000 µL pipet is used.'),q('SDS-PAGE separates proteins by:','Size','Color','Charge only','Shape only','SDS gives uniform negative charge.'),q('A diluted sample reads 0.4 mg/mL from the curve after a 1/50 dilution. The original concentration is:','20 mg/mL','0.008 mg/mL','0.4 mg/mL','50 mg/mL','0.4 × 50.'),q('Beer’s law states that absorbance is proportional to:','Concentration','Temperature','Volume only','Time','This allows standard curves.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain why protein shape matters, then describe how to perform the Bradford assay in HOSA Skill V and calculate the original concentration of a sample that reads 0.75 mg/mL on the standard curve after a 1/50 dilution.',rubric:[['Structure and function',['primary','secondary','tertiary','shape','denatur']],['Skill V steps',['2 µl','98 µl','standard','20 µl','1 ml','bradford']],['Measurement principle',['595','coomassie','standard curve','absorbance','blank']],['Calculation with dilution factor',['37.5','50','dilution factor']]],model:'A protein’s amino acid sequence folds into secondary structures and a tertiary shape, and sometimes quaternary assemblies, and that shape determines function, so denaturation by heat or pH destroys activity. For Skill V, I would wear PPE, make 1/50 dilutions by mixing 2 µL of each sample with 98 µL of 1x PBS, label cuvettes for a blank and seven standards, add 20 µL of PBS to the blank and 20 µL of each standard with clean tips, add the diluted samples, then add 1 mL of 1x Bradford reagent to each and mix gently. Coomassie dye turns blue when bound to protein, so after zeroing with the blank, I would read absorbance at 595 nm and plot a standard curve. A diluted reading of 0.75 mg/mL multiplied by the dilution factor of 50 gives an original concentration of 37.5 mg/mL.'},sources:[RULES,NCBI_PROT,BIORAD]};

export const B10={title:'Immunology and the ELISA',hook:'Many pregnancy tests, HIV screening tests and food allergen tests rely on the same idea: an antibody grabs a specific molecule, and an enzyme attached to an antibody turns a colorless solution blue. The enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay, or ELISA, harnesses the immune system’s precision for diagnosis.',objectives:['Describe antigens, antibodies and the basic immune response.','Distinguish polyclonal and monoclonal antibodies and their biotechnology uses.','Explain direct, indirect and sandwich ELISA formats and the role of controls.','Perform a qualitative ELISA following HOSA Skill VIII.'],sections:[
['Antigens and antibodies',[
'An **antigen** is any molecule that the immune system can recognize, often a protein or polysaccharide on a pathogen. **Antibodies** (immunoglobulins) are Y-shaped proteins made by **B cells** (as **plasma cells**). Each has two identical **heavy chains** and two identical **light chains** joined by disulfide bonds; the tips of the Y form **antigen-binding sites** in the **variable region**, and the stem is the **constant (Fc) region**. An antibody binds a specific part of an antigen called an **epitope**. The five classes are IgG (most abundant in blood, crosses the placenta), IgM (first produced, pentamer), IgA (in secretions), IgE (allergies and parasites) and IgD (on B cells).',
'The **primary immune response** after first exposure is slower and smaller; the **secondary response** on re-exposure is faster and larger because of **memory cells**, the basis of vaccination. Helper T cells coordinate the response, and cytotoxic T cells kill infected cells.',
match('Immunology terms',[['Molecule recognized by the immune system','Antigen'],['Specific region of an antigen bound by an antibody','Epitope'],['Cells that secrete antibodies','Plasma cells'],['First antibody class made in a response','IgM'],['Antibody class involved in allergies','IgE'],['Region of the antibody that binds antigen','Variable region']])]],
['Polyclonal and monoclonal antibodies',[
'**Polyclonal antibodies** come from many different B-cell clones and recognize multiple epitopes on an antigen; they are often produced by immunizing animals. **Monoclonal antibodies** come from a single B-cell clone and recognize one epitope. They were first made by fusing antibody-producing B cells with myeloma (cancer) cells to create **hybridomas** that grow indefinitely, a method developed by Köhler and Milstein in 1975. Today many therapeutic monoclonal antibodies are humanized or fully human and are produced in mammalian cells. Their uses include cancer therapy, autoimmune disease treatment, diagnostics such as rapid tests, and research tools.',
sort('Polyclonal or monoclonal?',['Polyclonal','Monoclonal'],[['Recognizes many epitopes on an antigen',0],['Produced from serum of an immunized animal',0],['Comes from a single hybridoma clone',1],['Recognizes exactly one epitope',1],['Used as a targeted cancer therapy',1]])]],
['How an ELISA works',[
'An **ELISA** detects an antigen or antibody using an antibody linked to an **enzyme**, usually horseradish peroxidase. Wells of a plastic microplate bind proteins. In a **direct ELISA**, antigen is attached to the well and an enzyme-linked primary antibody detects it. In an **indirect ELISA**, an unlabeled primary antibody binds the antigen and an **enzyme-linked secondary antibody** binds the primary antibody, amplifying the signal. In a **sandwich ELISA**, a capture antibody on the well grabs antigen from the sample, and a second enzyme-linked antibody binds another epitope. After each binding step, **washing** removes unbound material. Finally, a colorless **substrate** is added; the enzyme converts it to a colored product, usually blue. Color means the target was present.',
'**Controls** make results interpretable: a **positive control** contains the target and must turn color; a **negative control** lacks it and must stay colorless. Running each in **triplicate** shows consistency. A **qualitative** ELISA gives yes/no answers; a **quantitative** ELISA compares color intensity to a standard curve.',
order('Steps of an indirect ELISA',['Antigen or sample binds to the well','Wash away unbound material','Add primary antibody that binds the antigen','Wash','Add enzyme-linked secondary antibody','Wash','Add substrate; color develops where the target is present'])]],
['Qualitative ELISA (HOSA Skill VIII)',[
'HOSA Skill VIII uses a nine-well format: **three positive control wells**, **three negative control wells** and **three sample (S) wells**. After PPE and labeling, the competitor adds reagents to the correct wells with a **20–200 µL micropipet set to 50 µL**, a proper tip and bubble-free transfers. Samples are transferred **50 µL** into only the three S wells, incubated at room temperature (2 minutes in the HOSA scenario) and the **wash protocol** performed as directed (two times after the sample). **50 µL of enzyme-linked antibody (ELA)**, the secondary antibody, is added to all nine wells, incubated, and the wells washed **three times**. Finally **50 µL of enzyme substrate (SUB)** is added to all nine wells and color is observed, followed by the standard clean-up sequence. Washing typically means tipping out the wells onto paper towels and refilling with wash buffer; careless washing that splashes between wells causes false positives.',
kase('Read the ELISA plate',{a:{text:'After substrate is added, the three positive wells turn blue, the three negative wells stay clear and all three sample wells turn blue. What is the result?',choices:[['Positive: the sample contains the target','b','Correct. Controls worked and the sample matches the positive control.',true],['Invalid: too many blue wells','b','Blue positive controls are expected; the test is valid.',false]]},b:{text:'In another run, one negative-control well turns blue. What is the most likely cause?',choices:[['Contamination or inadequate washing, so the run may need repeating','c','Correct. Negative controls should stay colorless.',true],['The negative control contained the target by design','c','Negative controls must lack the target.',false]]},c:{text:'The lab reviews its washing technique, repeats the assay with fresh tips for every well, and the negative controls stay clear.',end:true}})]],
['Applications of immunology in biotechnology',[
'Immunological tools are everywhere in health care: **lateral flow** rapid tests (home pregnancy and COVID-19 antigen tests) use antibodies on a strip; ELISAs screen donated blood for infections and measure hormones; **flow cytometry** uses fluorescent antibodies to count immune cells; **immunohistochemistry** stains tissues for cancer diagnosis. Therapeutically, monoclonal antibodies block molecules such as tumor necrosis factor in autoimmune disease or flag cancer cells for destruction, and **vaccines**—including recombinant protein and mRNA vaccines—train the immune system without causing disease.',
flip('ELISA vocabulary',[['Primary antibody','Binds the target antigen'],['Secondary antibody','Enzyme-linked antibody that binds the primary antibody'],['Substrate','Colorless chemical the enzyme converts to color'],['Wash step','Removes unbound material between steps'],['Positive control','Contains target; must change color'],['Negative control','Lacks target; must stay colorless']])]],
["Vaccines and immune memory",["Vaccines train the adaptive immune system safely. **Inactivated** vaccines use killed pathogens; **live attenuated** vaccines use weakened pathogens; **subunit and recombinant** vaccines use purified proteins, sometimes made in yeast or cell culture, such as the hepatitis B vaccine; **toxoid** vaccines use inactivated toxins; and **mRNA** vaccines deliver instructions for cells to make a viral protein briefly. In each case, B and T cells respond, and **memory cells** produce a faster, stronger secondary response on later exposure.","ELISAs and related immunoassays are used to measure vaccine responses by detecting antibodies in blood, and to monitor manufacturing quality by detecting product proteins and contaminants. Understanding antibodies therefore connects diagnostics, therapeutics and prevention—three of the largest areas of the biotechnology industry."]]
],takeaways:['Antibodies are Y-shaped proteins with variable regions that bind epitopes; IgG is most abundant, IgM first, IgE in allergy.','Monoclonal antibodies come from one clone (hybridomas) and bind one epitope; polyclonal bind many.','ELISA: binding, washing, enzyme-linked antibody, substrate color; Skill VIII uses 50 µL volumes, triplicate controls, and wash 2 then 3 times.'],terms:[['Epitope','The specific part of an antigen an antibody binds.'],['Monoclonal antibody','Identical antibodies from a single B-cell clone.'],['ELISA','Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay that detects antigen or antibody with an enzyme color reaction.']],mcqs:[q('The part of an antigen that an antibody binds is the:','Epitope','Fc region','Light chain','Plasmid','Epitopes are specific binding sites.'),q('Which cells secrete antibodies?','Plasma cells','Red blood cells','Neutrophils','Platelets','Plasma cells come from B cells.'),q('Monoclonal antibodies are produced from:','A single B-cell clone, historically hybridomas','Many different B-cell clones','Red blood cells','Bacterial plasmids only','They recognize one epitope.'),q('In an indirect ELISA, the enzyme is linked to the:','Secondary antibody','Antigen','Substrate','Well plastic','This amplifies the signal.'),q('What does color development in a sample well indicate?','The target is present','The test failed','The well was not washed','The substrate is expired','The enzyme converts substrate to color.'),q('In HOSA Skill VIII, what volume is used for samples, ELA and substrate?','50 µL','5 µL','500 µL','1 mL','A 20–200 µL pipet is set to 50 µL.'),q('Why are wells washed between ELISA steps?','To remove unbound material and prevent false positives','To add color','To cool the plate','To denature antibodies','Washing improves specificity.'),q('A negative control well turns blue. This suggests:','Contamination or inadequate washing','A valid positive result','The positive control failed','Correct technique','Negative controls must stay colorless.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how an indirect ELISA detects an antigen, describe how to perform HOSA Skill VIII with correct controls and volumes, and interpret a plate in which positive controls are blue, negative controls are clear and sample wells are clear.',rubric:[['Antibody–antigen principle',['antigen','primary','secondary','enzyme','substrate']],['Skill VIII steps and volumes',['50 µl','three','wash','ela','sub','triplicate']],['Role of controls',['positive control','negative control','valid']],['Interpretation',['negative','not present','clear','valid']]],model:'In an indirect ELISA, antigen in the well binds a primary antibody, an enzyme-linked secondary antibody binds the primary antibody, and after washing, the enzyme converts a colorless substrate to a blue product wherever the antigen is present. For Skill VIII, I would wear PPE, label nine wells as three positive controls, three negative controls and three samples, add 50 µL of each reagent with a 20–200 µL pipet and fresh tips, incubate, wash two times after the sample, add 50 µL of enzyme-linked antibody to all wells, wash three times, then add 50 µL of substrate and observe color before cleaning up. The positive controls turning blue and negative controls staying clear show the test is valid. Clear sample wells mean the sample is negative: the target was not detected.'},sources:[RULES,NCBI_ELISA,NIAID_AB,BIORAD]};
